00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.040 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Julius Stevens an assistant professor  of history at Yale University she is an  historian of modern South Asia and her  research focuses on how law has shaped  religion family and economy in colonial  and postcolonial South Asia and in the  wider Indian diaspora she teaches  courses on modern South Asia Islam  gender and colonialism today we talked  with professor Stevens about the book  manuscript she is currently working on  tentatively entitled governing Islam law  Empire and secularism in South Asia  welcome professor Stevens thanks for  having me on the show of course so let's  begin with a little overview of your  book tell us about it great so Marilyn  my book m is looking at the encounter  between colonial law and Islam in South  Asia what we today think of as Pakistan  India and Bangladesh what I'm trying to  do in the book is to both tell a story  about how the official system of  colonial law approaches this body of  religious law centered on Islam but then  the other thing I'm trying to do in the  book is to think about law more broadly  and to think about how law is being  debated and practices practice not just  in the official court system but also  outside the courts in newspaper columns  a lot of newspapers in colonial India  had a special column devoted to the  court system when trials were being held  crowds would gather outside the courts  waiting to hear the decision so there's  this enormous public interest I'm in the  law that I'm trying to bring in to the  field of legal history but then also  especially with Islamic law there  continues to be this really rich world  of what I call vernacular legal culture  in which in madrassas scholars are  debating topics related to Islamic law  issuing fatwas  pamphlets legal tracks so it's sort of  the book is tracing on these parallel  worlds of law and kind of out of that  kind of broader notion of the law I'm  telling I'm two entangled stories and so  the first is about what kind of happens  at the official level of the overarching  structures of the colonial legal system  and I argue in the book that colonial  law really frames religion in a new way  religion in general and Islam in  particular and the colonial legal system  has certain structures in place that  kind of do this work and one of the most  important of these is something called  religious personal law which basically  is how the legal system incorporates  religious laws so religious law is  administered through the colonial courts  but only in a really specific delineated  set of cases and this kind of box that's  put around religion by the law really  links it exclusively with cases  involving the family and cases involving  ritual practice and what I argue in the  book is that this kind of legal framing  basically strongly associates religion  with the family and particularly with a  family that's envisioned as traditional  patriarchal and also associate Sri  legend with irrational beliefs and  rituals that are considered to be kind  of impenetrable to kind of rational  thought and so that these particular  legal structures I argue are creating a  version of secularism that has a kind of  particular colonial genealogy so my book  is telling a story that is in some ways  a global story across the world in the  19th and 20th century religion is  merging as this kind of distinct social  category that is separate from secular  spheres like politics and  economy but what I'm showing on top of  that in the book is that there's a kind  of different genealogy of that process  that unfolds in a colonial context so in  metropolitan Britain the religious and  secular are also coming untangled but  the in the 19th century they continue to  be largely seen as compatible and that's  that's not true at least at the level of  official discourse in in colonial India  so that's the kind of overarching story  so you've told us about the more  official law that was going on can you  tell us a little bit about the  vernacular the more commonplace things  that were happening yeah great because I  think this is really a kind of important  part of the story because in a sense you  know I think law always has kind of  multiple sides of the story I mean if  you think about what happens in courts  different arguments by against each  other and so kind of by broadening the  range of law that I'm looking at what I  show is that when we turn to kind of the  everyday practice of law both inside and  outside the courts that this kind of  official narrative of binary division  between the religious and the secular  really breaks down and is disrupted and  in everyday practice and so what I'm  trying to do in the book is tell those  two stories of how you have this  overarching structure of binaries but  then when you turn to the kind of the  lived life of legal practice you have  this richer story that adds a lot of  complexity ok so can you give us a  specific example that showcases that and  yeah so I work with a lot of cases  involving marriages and officially  marriage is part of this domain of  religious personal law but when you have  a feuding couple come into court what  you often find is they're arguing about  their domestic arrangements the wife is  moved out the husband wants you to move  back in but they're also arguing about  their economic relationships about you  know whose monies is whose whose  property came from which side of the  family and so it's really both  and an economic dispute and when the  courts actually have to you know deal  with this particular couple they find it  much more difficult to actually totally  untangle religious laws from secular  laws governing things like debts and  contracts okay so in that in a  particular case what would actually  happen I mean what would be the outcome  yeah so the the courts officially you  know create a decision where they try to  still kind of parse ok this is the  religious aspects of the case this is  the economic aspects of the case I have  literally cases that get kind of divided  in half even though they involve a  single couple but then you know often  what actually happens to the couple is  more complex than that maybe they  actually end up resolving their dispute  outside the courts by bringing family  members in and that's where the kind of  story and the divide often gets  complicated right ok so what led you to  write the book so I think for a long  time I've been struck and and troubled  by the way in which Islam in kind of  popular perceptions both in South Asia  and in the u.s. tends to be portrayed  with a kind of repetitive set of troubs  so we're constantly bombarded with  images of Islam oppressing women we  encounter the words fanaticism and Islam  almost used as synonyms and what I  really wanted to do with this book is to  kind of think about those tropes more  historically because I think I'm kind of  seeing how they've evolved through the  process of history actually allows us to  kind of complicate and and question the  tropes that we kind of encounter in our  daily lives ok and how did you do the  research for the book so this was a  multi-site cited research project I did  a lot of work in archives in South Asia  and in the UK but i think the kind of  most fun and interesting part of my  research really was trying to find  sources that would speak to this  vernacular legal culture and to do that  i ended up traveling to a lot of smaller  libraries in south asia that had  specialized collections on islam and  urdu language materials and you know  often this is  small library but they're holding you  know the only copy of a book or  newspaper and so it's really you know a  great privilege to be able to work with  these materials which are also often in  very delicate condition but you know I  think also interestingly i also found a  decent amount of material online because  addresses and islamic organization some  of the texts i study still have a real  kind of life today in kind of ongoing  debates about islamic law so what was  your what was one of the more  interesting or surprising cases that you  came across so i think probably one of  the most fun set of cases that i worked  with was a series of cases involving  inheritance disputes within households  of prostitutes and you know prostitutes  don't actually often come up in the kind  of official record sometimes they hide  but they in colonial India actually some  of these women really have a very  significant amount of property and so in  the end to deal with that property they  do come into the legal records and so  you know in addition to having you know  raising really interesting questions  about the law you get this kind of  window into these alternative households  of women who are kind of set up their  family firm as a group of women working  in the sexual labor do you find that the  law is equitable toward women or is it  more biased towards males so I mean this  is a really common perception about  Islamic law that it is patriarchal but I  think if you think about the 19th  century in fact Islamic law provides a  far more protection of a married woman  can actually owned her own property  under Islamic law and that doesn't  happen until later in the 19th century  in the case of British women so in fact  you have Muslims who are saying look you  know our laws are actually more  progressive when you think about gender  equality and certainly in terms of  property that's I think a very strong  argument  and your book is very innovative in a  certain way let's talk about that um  yeah so I think I'm trying to innovate  it at two levels one about methodology  and the other about kind of an argument  and in terms of methodology you know and  I talked about this a little bit before  it's really trying to bring together two  stories that have been told by other  scholars as really separate one so on  the one hand telling a story about  colonial law and colonial governance but  on the other hand it's looking at kind  of the intellectual and cultural life of  Muslims and in South Asia and I think  when you bring those two stories  together and look at how they're  interacting really interesting things  happen but in terms of the argument you  know there's a whole kind of world of  scholarship about secularism out there  but both in the in most the scholarship  as well as in kind of popular  understandings of secularism we really  think about it as something that first  evolved in Europe and then only later  kind of got exported to Asia the Middle  East but what I'm trying to show in the  book is that actually particular  variants of secularism emerged first and  in their most exaggerated form in fact  in this colonial context oh very  interesting and then ultimately what do  you conclude in your book yeah so I  think you know by basically looking at  how these binaries are historically  constructed I'm making a whole range of  arguments intervening with the  scholarship on South Asia and on Islamic  law about issues of chronology about how  we understand certain Islamic legal  concepts but I think today actually what  might be more helpful to talk about is  kind of what I think my book is saying  to a more general public I think what  i'm showing is that a lot of the ways  that we think about islam actually  emerged through a specific history of  colonialism and that if we understand  that history we can actually start to  think outside of the ways in which Islam  and secularism often positioned in a  binary relationship of opposition  and interestingly you know in an hour's  so I'm actually going into the classroom  to teach my last class in a course on  political Islam and you know I think  really this comes out in my teaching as  well that I'm trying to get students to  see the ways in which you know they have  been grown up in an environment in which  Islam and the West are often you know  spoken about as these two separate and  opposing entities but as a historian  what I try to show them is that Islam is  hugely internally diverse and the West  is hugely internally divorced French  people in Americans understand  secularism in really profoundly  different ways and then when we  appreciate that internal diversity we  start to see Islam in the West not as  locked in a kind of timeless  relationship of opposition but having  had this very complex in varied set of  mutual entanglements very good thank you  so much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work great Thank  You Marilyn for more information about  professor Stevens and her research  please visit our website at yale edu  slash macmillan report be sure to join  us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale

